#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111491} :activity/id #uuid "f8c81811-3f72-5396-b8eb-321dbcd75f82" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111879 :tutorial/content "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}.$ So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left( {\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm." :tutorial/id #uuid "bcae8943-0768-5280-ad4d-a67f04877fbe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2794 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e9f045cc-a10e-5c66-b731-eb57c6883345" :step/math-academy-id 14453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110494 :question/id #uuid "7c937587-dc81-5ee9-aa66-a509a87433e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4067" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right) - 1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34f4b65e-b93a-51e8-b943-b3b53d47fffb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186653068}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "b19bc194-a2b5-5800-8dae-7a6d496cb4da" :step/math-academy-id 14454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110594 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110495 :question/id #uuid "45d74b50-fd9d-5d18-835d-a7e140278a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-4069" :question/problem "Given that $3^{2x+3}=5,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Taking $\\log_3$ of both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n   \t3^{2x+3} \t\t\t\t&= 5 \\\\[3pt]\n\t\\log_3 \\left(3^{2x+3}\\right)\t&= \\log_3 (5) \\\\[3pt]\n\t2x+3 \t\t\t\t\t&= \\log_3 (5) . \\\\[3pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx -0.768\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n**Note:** We used the change of base formula to rewrite the right-hand side, as many calculators can only compute base $10$ logarithms."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7784d8-f6a4-5e45-b03e-d76a27372c48" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049879}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607761} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607757}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "8529df34-5e48-53b8-8d64-f02185cbc66e" :step/math-academy-id 14465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110595 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110496 :question/id #uuid "5d31a3e8-aae6-50b5-9ce1-65cef1d8395e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4071" :question/problem "Given that $3\\cdot 6^{x} = 9,$ find the value of $x$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {6}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {6}^{x} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take $\\log_{6}$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n   \t6^x \t\t\t\t\t&= 3 \\\\[3pt]\n\t\\log_6 \\left(6^x\\right)\t&= \\log_6 (3) \\\\[3pt]\n\tx \t\t\t\t\t&= \\log_6 (3)\\\\[3pt]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using the change of base formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\log(3)}{\\log(6)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above on a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx \\approx 0.613\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d076e5d-9d08-5355-b813-d1749feb0803" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607797} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607791}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"} :step/id #uuid "f8398d77-d45f-5c49-9a78-fef3d56b0cd8" :step/math-academy-id 14467}] :activity/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using Logarithms" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110848}]}
